  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 233 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nWednesday through Monday:\n\nHigh pressure continues to migrate eastward Wednesday through \nWednesday night, keeping southern Wisconsin dry with generally\nlight northeasterly winds. Highs in the mid to upper 60s and lows\nin the upper 40s to low 50s are expected.\n\nAs high pressure exits into early Thursday morning, two separate\nlow pressure systems approach the Upper Midwest. The first system\nejects from the southern Plains into the Middle Mississippi\nValley Thursday afternoon, bringing warm frontal precipitation to\nfar southern Wisconsin throughout the day. The second system will\nprogress through southern Ontario Thursday afternoon, phasing \nwith the first system and bringing widespread showers with\nisolated embedded thunderstorms to the remainder of Wisconsin by\nThursday evening. No severe weather is expected at this time with\nminimal instability and mainly broad forcing. These systems \ncontinue to interact and move eastward, phasing into a single \nsystem and deepening rapidly over southeastern Ontario Friday \nmorning. This will allow for the development of a deep adiabatic \nlayer across southern Wisconsin and plenty of mid-level (925-700 \nmb) winds to mix down and produce wind gusts up to 40 mph late \nThursday night into Friday, especially after precipitation ends.\n\nPrecipitation ends quickly into early Friday morning, with\nnorthwesterly winds continuing to gust 30 to 40 mph into at least\nmidday Friday as strong high pressure from the northern Canadian\nPrairies quickly builds southeastward into the Plains and \nMississippi Valley. This will also lead to highs only in the low\nto mid 50s as skies clear. Winds will gradually diminish Friday\nafternoon through Friday evening, becoming calm by late Friday\nnight. Lows in the 30s are expected, leading to the first frost\nand potentially localized freeze conditions into Saturday morning.\n\nHigh pressure propagates through the Ohio Valley Saturday and\nSaturday night, keeping winds light and southwesterly. Expect\nhighs in the 50s and lows in the 30s once again. As high pressure\nexits to the east, expect gusty southwesterly winds to develop\nSunday, bringing a return to highs in the 60s and lows in the 40s.\nSouthwesterly winds continue into Monday as low pressure occludes\nin the Hudson Bay, with some moisture return leading to shower\npotential by Monday afternoon.\n\nMH",
